  cardio: { title: 'Cardiovascular System', body: 'The cardiovascular system is a closed-loop pressure circuit: the heart (a 300-gram muscular pump) ejects roughly 70 mL of blood per beat, around 5 litres per minute at rest and up to 25 litres during intense exercise. Arteries carry oxygenated blood outward under high pressure; veins return deoxygenated blood under low pressure. The heart\'s two pumps work in parallel: the right side sends blood to the lungs (pulmonary circuit) and the left side sends it to the rest of the body (systemic circuit). The coronary arteries, which feed the heart muscle itself, are only 2–3 mm in diameter — which is why a small plaque can trigger a massive heart attack. Blood pressure is expressed as systolic/diastolic mmHg; 120/80 is textbook normal. Above 140/90 consistently constitutes hypertension, which silently damages artery walls for decades before causing strokes or kidney failure.' },
  resp: { title: 'Respiratory System', body: 'The respiratory system exchanges gases between the body and the atmosphere. Each breath draws air through the trachea, bronchi, and bronchioles into approximately 480 million alveoli — tiny sacs with walls one cell thick and a combined surface area of 70 m². Oxygen diffuses across this surface into capillary blood; CO2 diffuses out. The diaphragm, a dome-shaped muscle, is the primary driver of breathing — it contracts downward, increasing thoracic volume, creating negative pressure that draws air in. Respiration rate is controlled by chemoreceptors in the brainstem that respond primarily to CO2 levels (not O2, as commonly assumed). Asthma involves bronchoconstriction and inflammation of the airways; COPD involves progressive destruction of alveolar walls (emphysema) and chronic bronchitis — nearly always from smoking.' },
  digest: { title: 'Digestive System', body: 'The digestive system is a 9-metre tube from mouth to anus, performing mechanical and chemical breakdown of food, absorption of nutrients, and elimination of waste. The stomach produces hydrochloric acid (pH 1.5–3.5) and pepsin to begin protein digestion. The small intestine (6–7 m) does 90% of nutrient absorption via finger-like villi and microvilli that multiply surface area to approximately 200 m². The large intestine (1.5 m) reabsorbs water and houses the gut microbiome — approximately 38 trillion bacteria that synthesise vitamins K and B12, train the immune system, and produce short-chain fatty acids that nourish the colon lining. The liver processes everything absorbed from the gut via the portal vein, detoxifies compounds, and produces bile. Crucially: the liver receives 75% of its blood from the gut and 25% from the hepatic artery.' },
  neuro: { title: 'Nervous System', body: 'The nervous system is the body\'s communication and control network: 86 billion neurons, each forming up to 10,000 synapses. The central nervous system (brain + spinal cord) integrates information; the peripheral nervous system (somatic + autonomic) carries signals to and from the body. The somatic system controls voluntary movement; the autonomic system controls involuntary functions via two branches: sympathetic (fight-or-flight — raises heart rate, dilates pupils, diverts blood to muscles) and parasympathetic (rest-and-digest — slows heart rate, stimulates digestion). Neurons communicate via electrochemical impulses: an action potential travels along the axon at 1–120 m/s, and at the synapse, neurotransmitters (dopamine, serotonin, GABA, glutamate) cross the gap to receptor proteins. Myelin sheaths (degraded in multiple sclerosis) increase conduction speed 50-fold.' },
