  immune: { title: 'Immune System', body: 'The immune system operates in two layers. The innate immune system (first responders — neutrophils, macrophages, natural killer cells) responds within minutes to hours, recognises pathogen-associated patterns (not specific antigens), and initiates inflammation. The adaptive immune system (T and B lymphocytes) activates over days, generates exquisitely specific responses via antibodies and cytotoxic T cells, and forms immunological memory. Antibodies are Y-shaped proteins with variable regions that bind specific antigens — the lock-and-key principle. When B cells are activated, they can produce 10,000 antibody molecules per second. The MHC (major histocompatibility complex) is a set of molecules that display peptide fragments from inside cells on their surface — allowing cytotoxic T cells to identify and kill virus-infected or cancerous cells. Autoimmune diseases arise when this self/non-self discrimination breaks down.' },
  renal: { title: 'Renal System', body: 'Each kidney contains roughly 1 million nephrons — the functional filtering units. Together, they filter about 180 litres of blood per day, producing about 1.5 litres of urine. The filtration process is three-stage: glomerular filtration (pressure-driven removal of water, glucose, salts, waste), tubular reabsorption (recovery of 99% of filtered water and valuable solutes), and tubular secretion (active removal of drugs and extra waste). Kidneys regulate blood pressure via the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system; erythropoietin production (stimulating red blood cell synthesis); and vitamin D activation. Kidney function is measured by estimated GFR (eGFR): above 60 mL/min/1.73m² is normal; below 15 is kidney failure requiring dialysis or transplant. Diabetes and hypertension are the two leading causes of chronic kidney disease.' },
  musculo: { title: 'Musculoskeletal System', body: 'The skeleton provides structural support (206 bones in an adult, down from ~270 at birth as many fuse), calcium storage (99% of body calcium is in bone), and protection of soft organs. Bone is not static: osteoblasts continuously deposit new bone matrix while osteoclasts resorb old bone — a remodelling cycle completed in 3–6 months. Bone density peaks around age 30, then gradually declines — faster in women after menopause due to oestrogen loss. Skeletal muscle (600+ muscles) generates force via the sliding filament mechanism: actin and myosin filaments slide past each other when calcium binds troponin, shortening the sarcomere. Muscle contraction requires ATP; at maximal effort, ATP is consumed faster than mitochondria can produce it, requiring anaerobic glycolysis — which produces lactic acid, causing the "burn". Tendons transmit force; ligaments stabilise joints.' }
};

const diseaseData = {
  infect: { title: 'Infectious Disease', body: 'Infectious diseases are caused by pathogens: bacteria (living cells that replicate independently, killed by antibiotics), viruses (genetic material in a protein coat that hijack host cell machinery, requiring antivirals or vaccines), fungi (eukaryotes, treatable with antifungals), and parasites (complex organisms like Plasmodium falciparum, which kills ~600,000 people yearly via malaria). The key concept is the R0 (basic reproduction number) — the number of new infections one case generates in a fully susceptible population. Measles R0: 12–18. SARS-CoV-2 original: 2–3. Omicron: 8–15. R0 above 1 means exponential growth; herd immunity requires a fraction (1 − 1/R0) of the population to be immune. Routes of transmission determine prevention: respiratory (masks, ventilation), fomites (handwashing), faecal-oral (sanitation), vector-borne (insecticides, vaccines).' },
  cancer: { title: 'Cancer — Uncontrolled Cell Division', body: 'Cancer is fundamentally a disease of gene regulation. Normal cells divide on schedule, respond to signals to stop, undergo programmed death (apoptosis) when damaged, and cannot survive outside their tissue. Cancer cells acquire mutations that override all four of these controls. The Hallmarks of Cancer (Hanahan and Weinberg, 2000; updated 2022) describe 14 acquired capabilities, including: sustained proliferative signalling, evasion of growth suppressors, resistance to cell death, limitless replicative potential, angiogenesis (growing new blood vessels), and invasion/metastasis. Driver mutations accumulate over years — most cancers require 5–10 driver mutations before becoming malignant. The BRCA1/2 mutations are classic tumour suppressors: one functional copy is normal; lose the second copy ("two-hit hypothesis") and breast/ovarian cancer risk rises from ~12% to ~70%. Most cancers are caused by accumulated somatic mutations (not inherited) — the majority due to random replication errors, UV radiation, and carcinogens like tobacco smoke.' },
  cardio: { title: 'Cardiovascular Disease — The World\'s Biggest Killer', body: 'Cardiovascular disease (CVD) kills 17.9 million people per year — 32% of all global deaths. The core mechanism in most CVD is atherosclerosis: lipid-rich plaques accumulate inside arterial walls over decades, narrowing the lumen and making the wall stiff and prone to rupture. When a plaque ruptures, it triggers clot formation that can fully occlude the artery. In the coronary arteries: myocardial infarction (heart attack). In the cerebral arteries: ischaemic stroke. In the peripheral arteries: peripheral arterial disease. Risk factors are well-established: smoking (directly toxic to endothelium), hypertension (mechanical stress on vessel walls), LDL cholesterol (the lipid that accumulates), type 2 diabetes (damages endothelium via advanced glycation end-products), physical inactivity, and obesity. Statins reduce LDL cholesterol by 30–50% and reduce cardiovascular events by 25–35% — one of medicine\'s most robustly evidenced interventions.' },
  metabolic: { title: 'Metabolic Disease — Diabetes, Obesity, Metabolic Syndrome', body: 'Type 2 diabetes arises from insulin resistance (cells fail to respond to insulin) followed by beta-cell exhaustion (the pancreas cannot compensate by producing ever more insulin). The result is chronic hyperglycaemia, which damages small blood vessels (microvascular disease) and nerves (neuropathy). Long-term complications: diabetic retinopathy (leading cause of blindness in working-age adults), nephropathy (leading to dialysis), peripheral neuropathy (leading to amputation), and accelerated atherosclerosis. Metabolic syndrome — a cluster of hypertension, abdominal obesity, dyslipidaemia, and impaired fasting glucose — affects ~25% of adults globally and dramatically increases CVD risk. Obesity is not simply "excess calories": leptin resistance, gut microbiome composition, ultra-processed food palatability engineering, sleep deprivation, and genetic variants (FTO gene) all contribute. The idea that obesity is a simple failure of willpower is scientifically inaccurate.' },
  autoimmune: { title: 'Autoimmune Disease — When the Immune System Attacks the Self', body: 'Autoimmune diseases occur when the adaptive immune system fails to distinguish self from non-self, generating antibodies or cytotoxic T cells against the body\'s own tissues. There are over 100 recognised autoimmune conditions, collectively affecting 5–8% of the population — predominantly women (three-quarters of cases). Type 1 diabetes: T cells destroy insulin-producing beta cells. Rheumatoid arthritis: synovial joints are attacked, causing inflammatory destruction of cartilage and bone. Multiple sclerosis: myelin sheaths around CNS neurons are degraded, disrupting nerve conduction. Lupus (SLE): autoantibodies against DNA cause multi-organ inflammation. The hygiene hypothesis proposes that reduced early-life microbial exposure (from improved sanitation and antibiotic use) leaves the immune system under-trained in self-tolerance. This is supported by the fact that autoimmune disease is far rarer in rural low-income countries.' },
  genetic: { title: 'Genetic Disease — Inherited and De Novo Mutations', body: 'Genetic diseases arise from mutations in the germline (heritable) or arising de novo (new mutations in the egg or sperm or early embryo). Patterns of inheritance: autosomal dominant (one mutant copy sufficient — e.g., Huntington\'s disease, BRCA1 mutation), autosomal recessive (two mutant copies required — e.g., cystic fibrosis, sickle cell disease), X-linked (gene on X chromosome — e.g., Duchenne muscular dystrophy, haemophilia A, affects males predominantly), and mitochondrial (maternal inheritance only). Single-gene "Mendelian" diseases are individually rare but collectively significant: there are ~7,000 known Mendelian diseases, affecting 6–8% of people over a lifetime. Chromosomal disorders (Down syndrome = trisomy 21, Turner syndrome = 45,X) involve gain or loss of whole chromosomes. Next-generation sequencing has transformed diagnosis: a whole exome can identify causative mutations in ~25% of patients with undiagnosed diseases.' },
  ageing: { title: 'Ageing as Biology — Cellular and Systemic Decline', body: 'Biological ageing involves hallmarks identified in landmark papers: telomere shortening (each cell division trims 50–200 base pairs from chromosomal ends; after ~50 divisions in most cells, critically short telomeres trigger senescence), cellular senescence (cells stop dividing but persist, secreting pro-inflammatory cytokines — the SASP), epigenetic drift (methylation patterns change over time, altering gene expression), mitochondrial dysfunction, loss of proteostasis (protein misfolding accumulates — the mechanism in Alzheimer\'s, Parkinson\'s), and stem cell exhaustion. These hallmarks are connected: senescent cells accumulate partly because of telomere attrition; mitochondrial ROS damages DNA, accelerating senescence. The rate of ageing is partially modifiable: caloric restriction extends lifespan in every organism tested. Rapamycin (an mTOR inhibitor) extends lifespan in mice even when started late in life. Human trials are ongoing.' }
};

const imagingData = {
  xray: { title: 'X-ray (Radiograph)', body: 'X-rays use ionising electromagnetic radiation that passes through soft tissue but is absorbed by dense structures like bone and calcium deposits. The image is a 2D shadow. Dose per chest X-ray is roughly 0.1 mSv — equivalent to about 10 days of natural background radiation. Ideal for: fractures, pneumonia (lung consolidation appears white), pneumothorax (collapsed lung), foreign bodies. Not ideal for: soft tissue, brain, abdomen organs (poor contrast). Speed: 5 minutes from request to image. Key concept: radiodensity. Bone is white; air is black; soft tissue is grey. Calcified (old) TB lesions appear white on chest X-ray — a useful historical marker.' },
  ct: { title: 'CT Scan (Computed Tomography)', body: 'CT uses multiple X-ray beams rotated around the body, reconstructed by computer into cross-sectional images. Radiation dose is significant: a chest CT delivers ~7 mSv (equivalent to 3 years of background radiation). Advantages over plain X-ray: 3D reconstruction, excellent soft tissue detail with contrast, speed (a whole-body trauma CT takes ~3 minutes). Clinical uses: trauma (identifies internal bleeding, pneumothorax, fractures simultaneously), suspected pulmonary embolism, stroke (CT brain is first-line to exclude haemorrhage), abdominal pain, cancer staging. Contrast agents (iodine-based dyes) highlight vascular structures. Cumulative radiation is a concern — a person who has had 10 CT scans has increased lifetime cancer risk by approximately 1 in 2,000.' },
  mri: { title: 'MRI (Magnetic Resonance Imaging)', body: 'MRI uses a strong magnetic field (1.5–3 Tesla, ~30,000–60,000 times Earth\'s field) and radio waves to align and then perturb hydrogen nuclei in water molecules. The detected radio signal varies by tissue type and creates high-contrast images. No ionising radiation. Ideal for: brain and spinal cord (superior soft tissue contrast), musculoskeletal (cartilage, ligaments, menisci), liver, uterus, prostate. Not ideal for: lungs (low water content), bone cortex, acute trauma (slow — 30–60 minutes). Contraindicated in: ferromagnetic metal implants (some pacemakers, cochlear implants, certain clips). The noise (loud banging) is from Lorentz forces on gradient coils switching rapidly. fMRI (functional MRI) detects blood oxygenation changes as a proxy for neural activity — the basis of most brain imaging research.' },
  us: { title: 'Ultrasound', body: 'Ultrasound uses high-frequency sound waves (2–18 MHz) that reflect differently off tissues of different density. The transducer both emits and receives sound. No radiation. Real-time imaging. Cheap relative to CT/MRI. Ideal for: obstetrics (foetal anatomy, growth), abdominal organs (liver, gallbladder, kidneys), thyroid, blood vessels (Doppler mode detects flow), guided biopsy, cardiac function (echocardiography). Limitations: cannot penetrate gas (bowel, lungs) or bone; image quality operator-dependent; limited field of view. Point-of-care ultrasound (POCUS) — performed by the clinician at the bedside — has transformed emergency medicine: immediate assessment of heart function, pneumothorax, free fluid, DVT. A focused echocardiogram takes 3–5 minutes and can diagnose pericardial effusion or severe left ventricular dysfunction.' },
  pet: { title: 'PET Scan (Positron Emission Tomography)', body: 'PET detects metabolic activity by tracking a radiolabelled tracer (most commonly FDG — fluorine-18 labelled glucose) administered intravenously. Rapidly dividing cells (cancer, infected tissue, active brain regions) take up more glucose and appear as "hot spots". PET is almost always combined with CT (PET-CT) to overlay functional metabolic information onto anatomical structure. Dose: ~7 mSv. Primary uses: oncology (staging cancer, assessing treatment response — a tumour that stops lighting up after chemotherapy is responding), neurology (early Alzheimer\'s diagnosis via amyloid PET), cardiology (assessing myocardial viability). Limitations: expensive (~£1,500–£3,000 per scan), radiation, not widely available, and inflammation/infection can mimic cancer (false positives).' }
};

const modelData = {
  beveridge: { title: 'Beveridge Model', body: 'Named after William Beveridge, architect of the 1948 NHS. Government collects taxes, owns or contracts hospitals, employs or contracts doctors, and provides care free at the point of use. The logic: healthcare is a public good, like roads or defence. Examples: UK (NHS), Spain, Scandinavia. Advantages: universal coverage, no administrative complexity for patients, lower per-capita cost. Disadvantages: waiting times, political vulnerability (funding decisions tied to electoral cycles), potential for rationing. The NHS costs the UK roughly £3,000 per person per year — less than half the US per-capita spend — with comparable or better outcomes on most population health metrics.' },
  bismarck: { title: 'Bismarck Model', body: 'Named after 19th-century Prussian Chancellor Otto von Bismarck, who created the world\'s first social insurance system in 1883. Employers and employees contribute to non-profit sickness funds (Krankenkassen) that cover all citizens. Germany has over 100 such funds competing on service (not price or coverage, which are regulated). Similar systems in France, Belgium, Japan, Switzerland. Advantages: universal coverage, patient choice, private sector efficiency, political durability (not dependent on tax revenue). Disadvantages: more administrative complexity than Beveridge; cost-control harder. Germany spends ~$7,500/capita/year with near-universal coverage and short waiting times.' },
  natins: { title: 'National Insurance Model', body: 'A hybrid model where the government administers a single-payer insurance fund, but delivery is through a mix of public and private providers. Canada is the most studied example: provinces administer single-payer hospital and physician coverage (Medicare), but dentistry, prescription drugs, and long-term care are largely private. South Korea, Taiwan, and Australia (Medicare) are similar. Taiwan\'s National Health Insurance, implemented in 1995, covers 99.9% of the population and is widely regarded as the most technically successful healthcare financing reform in history — covering a previously uninsured majority within 12 months by building on existing infrastructure.' },
  oop: { title: 'Out-of-Pocket Model', body: 'In the out-of-pocket model, individuals pay directly for healthcare at the point of care, with minimal insurance or government involvement. This describes most healthcare in low-income countries and historically describes all healthcare. It is the world\'s most common system by population, though not by health expenditure. Consequences are brutal: healthcare becomes the leading cause of poverty and bankruptcy in many countries. In India, "catastrophic health expenditure" (spending >10% of household income on healthcare) affects ~55 million people per year. In the US, medical bills contribute to 60% of personal bankruptcies despite having formal insurance — largely due to gaps, copays, deductibles, and out-of-network billing. The WHO\'s goal is universal health coverage (UHC): everyone accessing quality healthcare without financial hardship.' }
};

const mhData = {
  depression: { title: 'Depression (Major Depressive Disorder)', body: 'Depression affects 280 million people globally — 5% of adults — making it the leading cause of disability worldwide (WHO, 2023). The DSM-5 requires at least 5 of 9 symptoms for 2+ weeks, one of which must be depressed mood or anhedonia (inability to feel pleasure). The "chemical imbalance" explanation (low serotonin = depression) is a significant oversimplification: the 2022 Moncrieff et al. umbrella review found no consistent evidence of lower serotonin in depressed people. Antidepressants do work — effect size vs placebo ~0.3 (NNT ~7–8). Cognitive-behavioural therapy has comparable efficacy for mild-moderate depression and better durability.' },
  anxiety: { title: 'Anxiety Disorders', body: 'Anxiety disorders are the most prevalent mental health conditions globally, affecting ~30% of people at some point in their lives. They include generalised anxiety disorder (GAD — excessive, uncontrollable worry), social anxiety disorder (~7% lifetime prevalence), panic disorder (recurrent unexpected panic attacks: sudden intense fear, racing heart, derealization), specific phobias, and agoraphobia. The core mechanism is the hyperactivation of the amygdala (fear response centre) and disruption of prefrontal cortex regulation of that response. CBT — particularly exposure therapy — is the most effective treatment for most anxiety disorders, with response rates of 60–80%. Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors (SSRIs) and SNRIs are effective pharmacological options. Benzodiazepines (diazepam, alprazolam) are effective short-term but addictive — not recommended for long-term use.' },
  schizo: { title: 'Psychotic Disorders — Schizophrenia', body: 'Schizophrenia affects ~1% of the population globally, with onset typically in late adolescence or early adulthood. It is characterised by positive symptoms (hallucinations — typically auditory, delusions — fixed false beliefs, disorganised thought), negative symptoms (flat affect, alogia, avolition, anhedonia), and cognitive impairment. The dopamine hypothesis (excess dopamine activity in mesolimbic pathways drives positive symptoms) explains why dopamine-blocking antipsychotics treat positive symptoms — but negative symptoms and cognitive impairment respond poorly. Schizophrenia is strongly heritable (heritability ~80%) but polygenic — no single gene explains more than a fraction of risk. First-episode psychosis requires urgent treatment; each additional year of untreated psychosis worsens long-term outcomes. Life expectancy is reduced by ~15 years, primarily from cardiovascular disease and suicide.' },
  bipolar: { title: 'Bipolar Disorder', body: 'Bipolar disorder involves episodes of mania (elevated/expansive mood, decreased need for sleep, grandiosity, impulsivity, pressured speech) alternating with depressive episodes. Bipolar I requires at least one full manic episode (lasting 7+ days or hospitalised). Bipolar II involves hypomanic (subthreshold) episodes and major depression. Affects ~2–4% of the population. The economic and functional burden is high: bipolar disorder accounts for more lost days of work than any other psychiatric diagnosis except schizophrenia. Lithium remains the most effective mood stabiliser after 70 years of clinical use — reducing both manic and depressive episodes, and uniquely reducing suicide risk by ~60% (NNT ~5). Mechanism is incompletely understood. Adherence is the major clinical challenge: during hypomanic or early manic phases, patients often feel well and stop medication.' },
  adhd: { title: 'ADHD and Autism Spectrum Disorder', body: 'ADHD (Attention Deficit Hyperactivity Disorder) affects ~5–7% of children and 2–5% of adults. It involves impaired executive function: sustained attention, impulse inhibition, working memory, and cognitive flexibility. ADHD is not a deficit of attention per se — people with ADHD can focus intensely on high-interest tasks (hyperfocus) but struggle to initiate or sustain attention on low-reward tasks. Stimulant medications (methylphenidate/Ritalin, amphetamines/Adderall) are among the most effective psychiatric medications — effect sizes of 0.8–0.9, comparable to the best antipsychotics. They work by increasing dopamine and noradrenaline in the prefrontal cortex. Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) affects ~1–2% of children. It involves differences in social communication and restricted/repetitive behaviours. ASD is highly heritable (~80%) but environmentally influenced. Crucially: vaccines do not cause autism. This claim originated in a fraudulent 1998 Lancet paper by Andrew Wakefield, who was struck off the medical register.' },
  ptsd: { title: 'PTSD — Trauma and Memory', body: 'PTSD (Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder) develops in approximately 10–20% of people exposed to traumatic events (lifetime prevalence ~3–4%). Core symptoms: re-experiencing (intrusive memories, nightmares, flashbacks), avoidance, negative alterations in cognition and mood, and hyperarousal (startle response, sleep disruption). The mechanism involves the amygdala failing to extinguish fear memories, combined with hippocampal damage from stress hormones (cortisol) impairing memory contextualisation. EMDR (Eye Movement Desensitisation and Reprocessing) and trauma-focused CBT are the most evidence-based treatments. SSRIs (sertraline, paroxetine) are approved pharmacological options. Critical: early intervention matters. Psychological debriefing (talking through the trauma immediately after) does not prevent PTSD and may worsen outcomes in some individuals.' },
  addiction: { title: 'Addiction — A Brain Disease', body: 'Addiction (substance use disorder) involves compulsive substance use despite harmful consequences, loss of control, and physical/psychological dependence. The neurobiological core is dysregulation of the mesolimbic dopamine system — the brain\'s reward pathway. Repeated substance use causes neuroadaptation: tolerance (requiring more for the same effect), withdrawal (rebound dysphoria), and sensitisation to cues. The "disease model" of addiction is well-supported neurobiologically: structural and functional brain changes are visible on imaging, and these partially normalise with sustained abstinence. This does not eliminate personal responsibility — it contextualises it. Effective treatments exist: opioid use disorder is best treated with buprenorphine or methadone (opioid agonist therapy), which reduces overdose deaths by ~50%. Alcohol use disorder: naltrexone, acamprosate, disulfiram. The abstinence-only approach has weaker evidence than harm-reduction approaches for most substances.' }
};

const emergencyData = {
  stroke: { title: 'Stroke — FAST', body: 'Stroke occurs when blood supply to part of the brain is cut off (ischaemic, 85% of cases) or when a blood vessel bleeds into brain tissue (haemorrhagic, 15%). Brain cells die at 1.9 million per minute during a large ischaemic stroke. FAST: Face (drooping on one side?), Arms (one arm drifts down?), Speech (slurred, confused?), Time (call emergency services immediately). Thrombolysis (tPA) must be given within 4.5 hours; mechanical thrombectomy within 24 hours for large vessel occlusion. Do not give aspirin until CT brain excludes haemorrhage. Do not let the person eat or drink — swallowing is often impaired after stroke, causing fatal aspiration.' },
  mi: { title: 'Heart Attack — Recognition and Response', body: 'Myocardial infarction (MI) occurs when a coronary artery is suddenly occluded, typically by rupture of an atherosclerotic plaque and clot formation, causing cardiac muscle death. Classic symptoms: central crushing chest pain radiating to left arm, jaw, or back; sweating; nausea; shortness of breath. But 30% of MIs — especially in women, diabetics, and elderly patients — present "atypically" with jaw pain only, fatigue, upper abdominal pain, or no pain at all (silent MI). What to do: call emergency services immediately. Chew 300mg aspirin (not swallow — chewing speeds absorption) if not contraindicated and if MI is suspected. Do not drive yourself to hospital. Time to reperfusion (primary angioplasty, "primary PCI") is the key determinant of outcome — every 30-minute delay costs ~1 year of life.' },
  anaphylaxis: { title: 'Anaphylaxis — Severe Allergic Reaction', body: 'Anaphylaxis is a life-threatening, systemic allergic reaction occurring within seconds to minutes of exposure to a trigger. The immune system releases massive quantities of histamine and other mediators, causing: urticaria (hives), angioedema (swelling, especially lips/throat), bronchospasm (wheeze, difficulty breathing), profound hypotension (vasodilation), and cardiovascular collapse. Common triggers: peanuts, tree nuts, shellfish, medications (penicillin, NSAIDs), insect venom, latex. Treatment: intramuscular adrenaline (epinephrine) 0.5 mg (adult) into the outer thigh — this is the only effective first-line treatment. Adrenaline reverses bronchospasm, increases blood pressure, and reduces mucosal oedema. Antihistamines (cetirizine, chlorphenamine) and corticosteroids do NOT treat acute anaphylaxis — they are adjuncts for preventing biphasic reactions. Lay the person flat with legs elevated. Call emergency services. EpiPen auto-injectors contain 0.3 mg epinephrine.' },
  choking: { title: 'Choking — Airway Obstruction', body: 'Choking occurs when a foreign body (most commonly food) partially or completely obstructs the airway. If the person can cough effectively, encourage coughing — do not intervene. If they cannot cough, are silent, cannot breathe, or are turning blue: deliver up to 5 firm back blows between the shoulder blades with the heel of the hand. If back blows fail, deliver up to 5 abdominal thrusts (Heimlich manoeuvre): stand behind, hands clasped below the sternum, thrust sharply upward and inward. Alternate 5 back blows with 5 abdominal thrusts. For infants under 1 year: use 5 back blows then 5 chest thrusts (NOT abdominal thrusts — risk of liver damage). If the person becomes unconscious: begin CPR — chest compressions may dislodge the object. Abdominal thrusts are contraindicated in pregnancy (use chest thrusts) and obesity.' },
  seizure: { title: 'Seizures — What To Do and What Not To Do', body: 'A seizure is abnormal synchronous electrical activity in the brain. Generalised tonic-clonic seizures involve muscle rigidity then rhythmic jerking, loss of consciousness, possibly cyanosis, and post-ictal confusion. Most seizures self-terminate within 1–3 minutes. What TO do: time the seizure; gently move sharp objects away; cushion the head; place in recovery position once jerking stops. What NOT to do: hold the person down (you cannot stop the seizure and risk injury); put anything in their mouth (not even a spoon — this is a myth and risks broken teeth and biting the rescuer); give water during the seizure. Call emergency services if: seizure lasts >5 minutes; the person does not regain consciousness; they sustain injury; it is their first seizure; or they have difficulty breathing afterwards (status epilepticus). In known epileptics with a brief typical seizure, calling an ambulance is not always necessary — follow the person\'s care plan.' },
  hypo: { title: 'Diabetic Emergencies — Hypo and Hyperglycaemia', body: 'Hypoglycaemia (<4 mmol/L in a treated diabetic) is the common acute emergency: symptoms include shaking, sweating, confusion, palpitations, and hunger. If conscious and able to swallow: give 15–20g fast-acting glucose (150–200 mL fruit juice, 3–4 glucose tablets, 5–6 Jelly Babies). Wait 15 minutes, recheck blood glucose. If unconscious: do not give anything by mouth. Call emergency services — IV glucose or glucagon injection is required. Hyperglycaemic emergencies (DKA — diabetic ketoacidosis) develop over hours to days with nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, fruity breath (acetone), confusion, and Kussmaul breathing (deep, rapid breathing compensating for acidosis). DKA is a medical emergency requiring hospital IV fluids, insulin, and electrolyte monitoring. HHS (hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state) is the type 2 equivalent: extreme dehydration and very high blood glucose without significant acidosis.' }
};

const futureData = {
  genomics: { title: 'Genomics and Personalised Medicine', body: 'Whole genome sequencing now costs under $200 (down from $3 billion for the first human genome in 2003). Pharmacogenomics is already clinical reality: variants in CYP2C19 determine whether clopidogrel is metabolically active; ~30% of people carry loss-of-function variants. The UK Biobank (500,000 participants with genotype + health records) has identified hundreds of disease-associated variants. Polygenic risk scores can predict lifetime risk of type 2 diabetes, coronary artery disease, and breast cancer more accurately than family history alone. Personalised medicine works best now for cancer (selecting targeted therapies based on tumour mutation profile) and drug dosing — not yet for population-level prevention. Key limitation: most variants explain tiny proportions of variance, and gene-environment interactions remain poorly characterised.' },
  crispr: { title: 'CRISPR — Gene Editing Therapy', body: 'CRISPR-Cas9 (Clustered Regularly Interspaced Short Palindromic Repeats) allows precise editing of DNA sequences in living cells. The Cas9 protein is guided to a specific genomic location by a "guide RNA" complementary to the target sequence, where it cuts the DNA double strand. In December 2023, the FDA and EMA approved the first CRISPR therapy: Casgevy (exa-cel), for sickle cell disease and beta-thalassaemia — editing patients\' own haematopoietic stem cells to reactivate foetal haemoglobin. One-time treatment with functional cure in >90% of patients in trials. Key remaining challenges: delivery (getting CRISPR into target cells in vivo), off-target edits (cutting wrong sites), and cost (Casgevy is priced at $2.2 million per patient). The 2018 case of He Jiankui creating CRISPR-edited embryos ("CCR5 deletion babies") to confer HIV resistance was widely condemned as premature, non-consensual, and ethically indefensible.' },
  ai: { title: 'AI in Medicine — Diagnosis, Drug Discovery, Limits', body: 'AI (primarily deep learning on large datasets) has shown remarkable diagnostic performance in narrow tasks. Google\'s DeepMind AlphaFold solved the 50-year protein structure prediction problem in 2020, predicting the 3D structure of virtually every known protein. In imaging: diabetic retinopathy detection from retinal photos (FDA-approved IDx-DR, 2018), skin cancer classification competitive with dermatologists, and chest X-ray abnormality detection. In drug discovery: Insilico Medicine has progressed an AI-generated drug candidate to Phase II trials (2023). The limitations are real: AI systems are trained on specific datasets and fail catastrophically when encountering out-of-distribution cases; chest X-ray models trained on US data perform poorly on African data. Clinical integration requires regulatory approval, liability frameworks, and evidence that AI improves outcomes — not just diagnostic accuracy in isolation.' },
  longevity: { title: 'Longevity Science — Extending Healthspan', body: 'The maximum lifespan of humans appears to be ~120–125 years (Jeanne Calment, 122 years, remains the verified record). But average healthspan (years of healthy life) lags life expectancy by ~10 years in most high-income countries. Cellular senescence is a major target: senolytic drugs (dasatinib + quercetin) clear senescent cells in mice, extending healthspan significantly. Rapamycin (mTOR inhibitor) extends lifespan in mice by 10–15% even when started in middle age. Metformin (cheap diabetes drug) correlates with reduced all-cause mortality in epidemiological studies and is now being trialled in non-diabetics (TAME trial). NAD+ precursors (NMN, NR) are popular supplements, with animal data suggesting improved mitochondrial function — but human RCT evidence is limited. Caloric restriction remains the most robust longevity intervention across species. The key uncertainty: do any of these translate to humans, and do they extend healthy years or just total years?' },
  amr: { title: 'Antimicrobial Resistance — The Slow-Moving Pandemic', body: 'Antimicrobial resistance (AMR) arises when bacteria evolve mechanisms to inactivate, efflux, or bypass antibiotics. Resistance mechanisms include: beta-lactamase enzymes that destroy penicillin rings, efflux pumps that expel antibiotics, and target site mutations. Resistance genes spread between bacteria via horizontal gene transfer (plasmids), not just vertical inheritance — explaining why resistance can appear globally within months of a new antibiotic being introduced. The ESKAPE pathogens (Enterococcus, Staphylococcus aureus, Klebsiella, Acinetobacter, Pseudomonas, Enterobacter) are the six most clinically dangerous resistant organisms. No new antibiotic class has been discovered since the 1980s. The pipeline is thin: only ~40 antibiotics are in clinical development. The WHO prioritised MRSA, CRE, and CRAB as critical-priority pathogens. Solutions require both new antibiotics and dramatically reduced unnecessary use — a global collective action problem.' }
};

/* ===== EXPLORER FUNCTIONS ===== */
function showSys(key, btn) {
  document.querySelectorAll('#sys-grid .sys-card').forEach(c => c.classList.remove('active'));
  btn.classList.add('active');
  document.getElementById('sys-title').textContent = sysData[key].title;
  document.getElementById('sys-body').textContent = sysData[key].body;
}

function showDisease(key, btn) {
  document.querySelectorAll('#disease-btns .exp-tab').forEach(b => b.classList.remove('active'));
  btn.classList.add('active');
  document.getElementById('disease-title').textContent = diseaseData[key].title;
  document.getElementById('disease-panel').querySelector('.muted-copy-large').textContent = diseaseData[key].body;
}

function showImaging(key, btn) {
  document.querySelectorAll('.exp-tab').forEach(b => {
    if (b.parentElement.contains(btn)) b.classList.remove('active');
  });
  btn.closest('.button-row').querySelectorAll('.exp-tab').forEach(b => b.classList.remove('active'));
  btn.classList.add('active');
  document.getElementById('imaging-title').textContent = imagingData[key].title;
  document.getElementById('imaging-body').textContent = imagingData[key].body;
}

function showModel(key, btn) {
  btn.closest('.button-row').querySelectorAll('.exp-tab').forEach(b => b.classList.remove('active'));
  btn.classList.add('active');
  document.getElementById('model-title').textContent = modelData[key].title;
  document.getElementById('model-body').textContent = modelData[key].body;
}

